#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183935 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fcba20f-b43b-558d-bd84-d76816d3186b" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Radical Expressions Using the Rules of Absolute Value"}] :question/id #uuid "d669aafc-c1f1-5acd-b7d8-5ff0f31178b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1361" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3}$." :question/worked-solution "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3} &= \\ \\sqrt{x \\cdot x^3} &= \\ \\sqrt{x^4} \\\\ &= \\mid x^2 \\mid \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid x^{2} \\mid &= x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183936 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184321 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2b7926-fa13-5636-9682-8fa382336be7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Cube Root Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "d6b143d1-7d3f-5836-8797-19cb37181541" :question/math-academy-id "e-1364" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{16x^2}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{4x}$." :question/worked-solution "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{16x^{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{4x} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{16x^{2} \\cdot 4x} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{64x^{3}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{(4x)^{3}} &=  \\\\\n&= 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184131 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37603549-5c55-5b9e-8d2d-2121db167d02" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Radical Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "cd3fba6a-bb16-51b1-b2ea-fb88656d5aa6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9979" :question/problem "Find an expression that's equal to $\\sqrt{5w^3} \\cdot \\sqrt{uv}$ by combining the radicals." :question/worked-solution "We apply the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{5w^{3}} \\cdot \\sqrt{uv} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{5w^{3} \\cdot uv} &=  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5uvw^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
